<div id="A1.SS1.SSS23.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.SSS23.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To avoid dogmatic debates, <span id="A1.SS1.SSS23.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">==</span> is only mutated to <span id="A1.SS1.SSS23.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">!=</span> and
<span id="A1.SS1.SSS23.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">===</span> only to <span id="A1.SS1.SSS23.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">!==</span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.SSS23.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.SSS23.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS1.SSS23.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Implementation.</span> This is implemented using AST-level arid node tagging, matching binary operators.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.SSS23.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.SSS23.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS1.SSS23.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Soundness.</span> This heuristic is not sound because it relies on the code
style recommendation on comparison operators.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS1.SSS24" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="A1.SS1.SSS24.6" class="ltx_text">A-A</span>24 </span>Acceptable Bounds</h4>

<div id="A1.SS1.SSS24.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.SSS24.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Gating a computed result into an acceptable bound by using Math.min, Math.max,
or constrainToRange of Ints, Longs, and friends is by design unlikely to change
behavior when one of the inputs is mutated. This is similar to
the Insensitive arguments heuristic, and resulting mutants are usually unproductive.</p>
</div>
